---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Questions about our approach" description="Practical answers about semantic modelling, existing systems, privacy, accuracy, AI, professional expertise and participation in the Smart Property Data Trust Framework.">
  <h1>Questions about our approach</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Shared meaning should make property information easier to work with. It should
    also be possible to question the approach before investing time in it. These answers
    distinguish the method we are developing, the website you can explore now, and the
    decisions that still need people to make them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="training">Do I need to understand ontologies to take part?</h2>
  <p>
    No. You can review a definition, explain a business rule, identify an exception or
    challenge a diagram using the language of your profession. Facilitators translate
    that knowledge into the formal model. Knowing that two dates mean different things,
    or that a proposed definition excludes a common transaction, is useful expertise
    whether or not you know the technical vocabulary. Start with the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/taking-part">participation guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="another-dictionary">Is this just another data dictionary?</h2>
  <p>
    A dictionary explains data elements. An ontology also makes the things those
    elements describe and their connections explicit. An amount can be connected to a
    particular valuation, its subject, purpose, date and source. The dictionary remains
    useful: it is one part of the <a href="/semantic-modelling/semantic-package">same
    semantic package</a>, alongside definitions, vocabularies and relationships.
    The aim is to make those views agree, rather than maintain competing specifications.
  </p>

  <h2 id="replace-systems">Will organisations have to replace their systems?</h2>
  <p>
    The method does not require everyone to adopt one database or application. An
    organisation can retain its own model and connect the information it exchanges to
    agreed meanings. Familiar JSON messages and APIs can remain delivery formats.
    Adapters and transformations still require engineering; an ontology is not an
    automatic compatibility layer. The benefit is a visible agreement against which
    that work can be designed and checked.
  </p>

  <h2 id="shared-database">Does linked data mean one shared database?</h2>
  <p>
    No. Linked data describes how information can use stable references and explicit
    connections. It does not prescribe one place where all records must be stored.
    A shared definition of “inspection date” can be used by many systems while their
    actual inspection records remain separately controlled. Decisions about hosting,
    exchange and access belong to the systems and agreements that use the model.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy">Does making the model open expose customer data?</h2>
  <p>
    Publishing a definition is different from publishing records about people or
    transactions. The public website presents modelling documentation and candidate
    structures; contributors should use fictional examples and the authorised private
    intake for permitted source material. An ontology can describe sensitivity, purpose
    and consent, but it does not itself enforce access. Follow the
    <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide/source-material-and-sharepoint">source-material
    guidance</a> before submitting anything.
  </p>

  <h2 id="one-definition">Why not agree one definition for the whole industry?</h2>
  <p>
    Sometimes one definition is exactly right. Sometimes it hides a distinction that
    matters: an asking price, a lending valuation and a rebuilding cost are all amounts
    associated with a property, but they support different decisions. We begin with
    the people who understand each meaning and agree the connections between contexts.
    The <a href="/semantic-modelling/bounded-contexts">common boundary</a> holds what
    genuinely needs a shared definition; it is not a destination for every unresolved term.
  </p>

  <h2 id="existing-standards">Why not import an existing ontology?</h2>
  <p>
    Existing standards are valuable sources of reusable terms and tested ideas.
    Reuse works when the meaning, scope and governance fit the question being answered.
    Importing an entire model before examining those questions can introduce assumptions
    a working group has never reviewed. We assess exact terms and uses, explain where
    they fit, and record where a local distinction or an explicit mapping is needed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accuracy">Does a valid model guarantee accurate information?</h2>
  <p>
    No. A check can establish that a date is present and correctly formatted without
    proving that an inspection happened on that date. Technical validation checks
    declared expectations; source evidence and practitioner judgement address different
    questions. Making those responsibilities visible helps people understand a result.
    It does not replace investigation, professional responsibility or the need to
    challenge an unreliable source.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">Is AI deciding what the standard means?</h2>
  <p>
    No. AI can help organise evidence, propose wording and identify apparent
    inconsistencies. Those are proposals for people to examine. Several models agreeing
    does not establish truth or grant authority. The accepted Property Pack authoring
    approach keeps machine-generated candidates subject to human review; the wider
    operating workflow continues to have its own recorded status. See
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-method">how we model</a> for the distinction.
  </p>

  <h2 id="disagreement">What happens when experts disagree?</h2>
  <p>
    First establish whether they are answering the same question in the same context.
    A difference may be legitimate rather than an error to eliminate. Record examples,
    evidence and the practical consequences of each interpretation. Where agreement is
    needed, the affected owners and OPDA governance must resolve it; software cannot
    settle the issue by counting comments. A draft can retain an open question without
    pretending that agreement already exists.
  </p>

  <h2 id="available">Is everything described here already built or approved?</h2>
  <p>
    No. You can <a href="/search">search and explore</a> generated model pages and the
    current Property Pack candidate. Page status distinguishes implementation, review
    and approval. Some delivery formats and expanded review facilities remain planned.
    The <a href="/development/outputs">outputs register</a> records that boundary.
    If you want to shape the work, <a href="/join">express interest in a working
    group</a>; registration is followed by human review, not automatic membership or
    decision rights.
  </p>
</Layout>
